{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 17, :comments ["<b>And I will take of the spirit that is upon you.</b> Scripture says: at the time I speak with you I will draw from the spirit that is upon you and place it upon them, and with this they shall be partners with you in bearing the burden of the people and you shall not bear it alone. This is the answer to what he said: ״לא אוכל אנכי לבדי לשאת״ — 'I am not able to bear it alone.' And what it says, ״ודברתי עמך שם״ — 'and I will speak with you there' — that is to say, the seventy will also hear. And the Holy One, blessed be He, gave him this counsel, that he should have help in bearing the burden of the people, and this was his reward, because he surrendered his soul for them in saying ״הרגני נא הרוג״ — 'kill me, I pray, and let me die'; and furthermore he said ״ואל אראה ברעתי״ — 'and let me not see my evil' — and this is an allusion, for he should have said ״ברעתם״ — 'their evil' — but he alluded to himself." "<b>And behold,</b> Moses was elevated by that counsel to a great degree, in his being likened to his Creator, blessed be He, who emanates an emanation of abundance upon the seventy heavenly princes who are His court — so did He do for Moses, that he should be the one who emanates upon the seventy elders below, who are the court of Moses, and he is the prince over them and supreme over all of them. And this emanation from Moses took place only at the hour when the Holy One, blessed be He, spoke with him, and this is the language of Scripture: ״ודברתי עמך שם ואצלתי״ — 'and I will speak with you there and I will take.' And so in the account of the deed: ״וידבר אליו ויאצל״ — 'and He spoke to him and He took' — for He, blessed be He, at the time of speaking with Moses, drew from the spirit that was upon him and placed it upon them. And this is the answer to what he said ״ומצא להם״ — that is to say: how do you doubt the matter whether I am able to provide for Israel to give them meat, not by way of miracle but by way of nature and chance — cattle and sheep and fish? And is not your spirit one alone, and I will make it suffice for the seventy elders! And if you ask what the matter was, it is possible that it was like the arrangement of the Sanhedrin: that the Nasi presides over the seventy, and the Av Beit Din sits at his right, and all the rest of the Sanhedrin sit before the two of them, and they sit in the form of a half-round threshing floor; and also like the procedure of semikhah for those who are ordained." "<b>And know</b> that the essential meaning of the term אצילות is drawing forth and increase, and this is the view of Onkelos, who translated ואצלתי as וארבי — 'and I will increase,' and likewise ויאצל as ורבי. And this is why in the account of the deed the term ויתן — 'and He gave' — is used, which is a term of increase, as in ״ועץ השדה יתן פריו״ (Leviticus 26) — this is instrumented increase and that is natural increase — and so too is the view of the men of language. And it is known that the term אצילות is said of a thing that is drawn and emanated from the power of the one who emanates, from his emanator, and the one who emanates lacks nothing. And our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: to what was Moses likened at that hour? To a lamp set upon a lampstand — all are lit from it and its light lacks nothing." "<b>And furthermore</b> they said in the Midrash: a parable — to what is the matter likened? To a man who had a orchard and entrusted it to a hired watchman. After some time the watchman said to him: I cannot guard it alone; rather, bring others to guard it with me. The king said to him: to you I gave it, and now you say, bring others to guard with me? I will heed you, but know that I am not giving the wage of guarding from my own — rather, from the wage I gave to you, from there they take their hire. Thus the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses when he said ״לא אוכל אנכי לבדי לשאת״: I gave you spirit and knowledge to provide for My children, and you had no need to seek others, so that you alone would be distinguished in that greatness; and now you seek others with you — know that from your own they take. Nevertheless Moses lacked nothing. You can know this, for after forty years the Holy One, blessed be He, said to Moses (Numbers 27): ״קח לך את יהושע בן נון איש אשר רוח בו וסמכת את ידך עליו ונתת מהודך עליו״ — and what is written? (Deuteronomy 34) ״ויהושע בן נון מלא רוח חכמה כי סמך משה את ידיו עליו.״ The Holy One, blessed be He, said: in this world the individuals prophesied, but in the time to come all Israel become prophets, as it is said (Joel 3): ״והיה אחרי כן אשפוך את רוחי על כל בשר ונבאו בניכם ובנותיכם זקניכם חלומות יחלמון בחוריכם חזיונות יראו.״" "<b>And you must</b> know further concerning the term אצילות, that the אצילות precedes בריאה, and the בריאה and the יצירה and the עשיה that are in all the work of creation are included within the power of the אצילות, for the אצילות precedes all of them. And Scripture called the seventy elders at the giving of the Torah, who died in the conflagration at Taberah (Exodus 24), ״אצילי בני ישראל״ — 'the nobles of the children of Israel' — because they attained knowledge of the אצילות and beheld its end. And the matter of אצילות is mentioned in the Writings and in the words of the Sages under varying names, all according to the context. For King Solomon, peace be upon him, designated it in the Song of Songs (3) by the name אפריון, made from the cedars of Lebanon — that is to say, a thing emanated from a thing; and the closest analogy is like lighting lamps from one lamp, all the lit lamps spreading and being drawn from that first lamp, with the light of the lamp lacking nothing — so too the one who emanates lacks nothing. And they also mentioned it there in that book under the name לקיטה — gathering — as he said (Song of Songs 6): ״וללקוט שושנים.״" "<b>And the masters of</b> truth call it by the name 'uprooting,' because it was emanated from something and uprooted from it; and this is what the Sages, of blessed memory, expounded on (Psalms 104:16): ״ישבעו עצי ה' ארזי לבנון אשר נטע״ — Rabbi Ḥanina said: they were like the horns of locusts, and the Holy One, blessed be He, uprooted them and planted them in the Garden of Eden. And its explanation: He uprooted them from it, and it says ״אשר נטע״ — 'which He planted' — and this is in the manner of what is said (Genesis 2:8): ״ויטע ה' אלהים גן בעדן״, and it did not mention the planting — from where it was. And Solomon, peace be upon him, came and explained: from the trees of Lebanon. (Song of Songs 3:9) Rabbi said: ״ישבעו עצי ה'״ — they shall be sated with their life, they shall be sated with their waters, they shall be sated with their planting. He mentioned the causes, which are one above the other; and because every planting needs watering, for it draws from the waters and the waters from the life and the life from the source of life, therefore he mentioned 'their life, their waters, their planting' — since when He likened the emanation of the beings to uprooting, He likened their creation and their growth to planting. And this is what they said: the Holy One, blessed be He, uprooted them and planted them in the Garden of Eden."], :book :numbers, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 11, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T14:31:39.009252274Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}